libusb: make cancel_transfer actually terminate under Emscripten/WebUSB.

Required for any USB device whose driver uses libusb's synchronous API with a
timeout -- which includes UHD's B2xx transport, and therefore the USRP B2xx
Source. Without this hunk a USRP hangs partway through device initialisation and
the tab must be reloaded.

WebUSB exposes no way to abort an in-flight transfer: once transferIn() has been
called its promise settles when the device answers, and never before. libusb
implements every synchronous-transfer timeout by calling the backend's
cancel_transfer and then waiting for completion. The stock Emscripten backend's
em_cancel_transfer is a no-op returning LIBUSB_SUCCESS, so a bulk IN on an
endpoint with no data never completes and never times out, and the caller blocks
forever.

That is not a corner case. UHD's libusb_zero_copy transport flushes its receive
endpoint during ordinary device setup with

    libusb_bulk_transfer(..., 10 /* timeout ms */)

in a loop whose only exit is LIBUSB_ERROR_TIMEOUT, so B2xx bring-up stops dead
at usb_zero_copy::make().

The fix cannot abort the transfer, so it stops *waiting* for it: cancel completes
the libusb transfer immediately, letting libusb's timeout and cancellation paths
make progress. libusb maps that to LIBUSB_TRANSFER_TIMED_OUT when the cancellation
came from a timeout (io.c, usbi_handle_transfer_cancellation) and
LIBUSB_TRANSFER_CANCELLED otherwise, which is what synchronous callers expect.

Completing the transfer is only half of it. The bytes the cancelled read will
receive are still coming, and simply dropping them loses data belonging to the
endpoint's stream. That does not show up as a lost read -- it shows up much later,
as a request that is never answered, because a stale read swallowed its reply.
Observed directly: with cancellation alone, a B2xx clears UHD's endpoint flush and
then fails in wait_for_ack, because the flush's ~100 outstanding reads consumed the
first CHDR control response.

So a cancelled read is orphaned rather than discarded: it stays queued against its
endpoint, and the next submission on that endpoint adopts it instead of issuing a
fresh transferIn. Ordering is preserved and no data is lost. Writes and control
transfers have nothing to hand on and are simply completed.

Adoption requires the two reads to be the *same length*: a read's size is fixed
when transferIn() is called, so handing a short one to a longer transfer truncates
the packet and desynchronises the endpoint. Mismatches are refused and a fresh read
is issued instead. Note that this makes a caller which cancels reads of one size
and then reads a different size unrecoverable by this layer -- UHD's endpoint flush
did exactly that, and is fixed separately in
uhd-frame-sized-endpoint-flush.patch.

Lifetime needs care too. Once libusb completes a transfer the caller may free it
while the browser callback still holds the usbi_transfer pointer, so the callback
consults a shared record before touching that memory. The same record tracks
whether a PromiseResult was ever placed in transfer_priv, since
em_clear_transfer_priv must not destroy one that was never constructed.

Applied by deps/fetch-deps.sh. Upstream libusb is v1.0.30.

It also registers the two embind types this backend passes across the JS boundary
(emscripten::val and memory_view<uint8_t>), which is only needed when libusb is
linked into an Emscripten *side module*. embind keys its registry on TypeID<T> --
&typeid(T) with RTTI -- and the registry is per JavaScript realm. The main module
registers its own set at startup and Emscripten replays that into every pthread,
but this file's typeinfo is a separate object from the main module's, so those
registrations do not answer lookups made from here and a val call fails with

    BindingError: _emval_take_value has unknown type N10emscripten11memory_viewIhEE

The registration has to live in *this* translation unit. Registering from a
neighbouring file in the same side module is not equivalent and was measured not
to work: libusb is compiled separately, with its own visibility flags, so its
typeinfo does not merge even with other objects in the same module. The ids
differed by millions.


